            "id": "4946230",
            "title": "The Fed May Be Forced Into An Irrelevant Hike",
            "description": "<html><body><p data-eci=\"true\"><figure class=\"getty-figure\" data-type=\"getty-image\"><picture> <img alt=\"Federal Reserve, Washington DC, USA\" data-id=\"459229485\" data-type=\"getty-image\" fetchpriority=\"high\" height=\"1024\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 132px), (max-width: 1199px) calc(100vw - 666px), (max-width: 1307px) calc(100vw - 708px), 600px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/459229485/image_459229485.jpg?io=getty-c-w630\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/459229485/image_459229485.jpg?io=getty-c-w1536 1536w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/459229485/image_459229485.jpg?io=getty-c-w1280 1280w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/459229485/image_459229485.jpg?io=getty-c-w1080 1080w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/459229485/image_459229485.jpg?io=getty-c-w750 750w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/459229485/image_459229485.jpg?io=getty-c-w640 640w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/459229485/image_459229485.jpg?io=getty-c-w480 480w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/459229485/image_459229485.jpg?io=getty-c-w320 320w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/459229485/image_459229485.jpg?io=getty-c-w240 240w\" width=\"1536\"/> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"> </p> <p class=\"item-credits\">Jeremy Edwards/iStock Unreleased via Getty Images</p></figcaption></figure></p> <p>Not only is price stability half of the Fed’s dual mandate, but Chairman Kevin Warsh has stated in unambiguous language that getting inflation to 2% is the Fed’s job. He is not going to pass the buck by blaming fiscal policy or oil prices. Yet, he may not have the tools to materially impact inflation in the near to medium term.</p> <p>This puts the Fed in a tight spot.</p> <p>They need to get inflation down and the market is demanding a hike, but will a hike actually combat inflation?</p> <p>This article will examine the mechanisms by which monetary policy impacts inflation and show that hikes might not be the inflation cure they have been in the past.</p> <p><strong>Old Fed-watching heuristics no longer apply</strong></p> <p>The general rule of thumb for monetary policy is:</p> <ul> <li>Higher rates reduce jobs and reduce inflation</li> <li>Lower rates increase jobs and increase inflation</li> </ul> <p>Thus, the Fed is constantly working with a tradeoff between its mandates. They can help employment or price stability, but not both.</p> <p>Former Fed Chair, Jerome Powell, consistently walked the market through this decision tree, transparently discussing whether he was more worried about jobs or inflation at any given time. With this handholding, the market could predict with near certainty whether the Fed would cut, hike or hold steady.</p> <p>Market implied odds going into a Fed meeting got it right nearly every single time with Powell.</p> <p>Kevin Warsh is intentionally opaque. He wants the market to directly respond to economic news rather than the market responding to how it thinks the Fed will respond to said news.</p> <p>The market has not yet gotten the memo and is still largely being driven by what it thinks the Fed will do. Right now, the market is fully convinced there will be a September hike. Implied odds of a 25-basis-point hike are at 92.7%.</p> <p><figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><picture> <span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"517\" data-og-image-facebook=\"true\" data-og-image-google_news=\"true\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"true\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"true\" data-og-image-msn=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"true\" data-width=\"966\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173481982212_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"A screenshot of a computer AI-generated content may be incorrect.\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"343\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173481982212_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173481982212_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173481982212_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173481982212_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173481982212_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"><span>CME Group</span></p></figcaption></figure></p> <p>I have no idea whether the Fed will hike or not but instead posit that it doesn’t make much of a difference.</p> <p>Specifically, a Fed hike is less relevant than normal for 3 reasons:</p> <ol> <li>Fed Funds rate may not impact the long end in the current environment</li> <li>A hike will not do much to combat inflation</li> <li>Interest rates are restrictive regardless of hike or hold</li> </ol> <p><strong>No parallel shift </strong></p> <p>The mechanism by which the Fed Funds rate reduces inflation is by making borrowing more expensive. The Fed Funds rate only directly impacts the short end of the curve, but historically it has often caused a parallel shift along the entire curve.</p> <p>In theory, the premium associated with duration risk would remain the same so the short end yielding 25 basis points more would roughly translate to the long end yielding 25 basis points more.</p> <p>However, in the current environment, the long end of the yield curve has moved up materially on fears of sustained inflation as well as mounting national debt and deficit.</p> <p>To the extent rate hikes are seen as deflationary, they could reduce inflation fears that are propping up the long-end yield. As such, we don’t think there would be a parallel shift in response to a Fed hike and that the long end could actually come down.</p> <p><strong>Hike may not be effective at stopping inflation</strong></p> <p>Inflation happens when demand exceeds supply. Interest rate hikes are quite effective at tamping down excess demand, but the current bout of inflation is not caused by broad-based demand. Instead, today’s slightly elevated inflation is largely a result of 2 factors:</p> <ol> <li>Supply shocks (oil in particular)</li> <li>Inelastic demand for AI infrastructure</li> </ol> <p>Rate hikes will do little to stop either of these.</p> <p>A higher Fed Funds rate primarily acts through big ticket expense items for which people and companies take on financing.</p> <p>The largest item is housing, but rates are already highly restrictive with the 30-year mortgage rate bouncing between 6% and 7% for most of the last 3 years.</p> <p><figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><picture> <span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"676\" data-og-image-facebook=\"true\" data-og-image-google_news=\"true\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"true\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"true\" data-og-image-msn=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"true\" data-width=\"1195\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474204147_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"A graph showing a graph AI-generated content may be incorrect.\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"362\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474204147_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474204147_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474204147_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474204147_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474204147_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"><span>TradingEconomics</span></p></figcaption></figure></p> <p>As mortgage rates went up in 2022, the volume of home sales dropped materially and has remained at minimal levels.</p> <p><figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><picture> <span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"565\" data-og-image-facebook=\"true\" data-og-image-google_news=\"true\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"true\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"true\" data-og-image-msn=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"true\" data-width=\"1180\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-1789417348193466_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"A screenshot of a graph AI-generated content may be incorrect.\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"306\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-1789417348193466_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-1789417348193466_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-1789417348193466_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-1789417348193466_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-1789417348193466_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"><span>TradingEconomics</span></p></figcaption></figure></p> <p>With housing already deeply depressed, it is unclear that further rate hikes will do much to demand.</p> <p>The area with excess demand is AI infrastructure and as measured by purchasing activity, it is extremely inelastic. Even as prices of turbines, processors, memory and chips have surged, the buying continues to accelerate.</p> <p>I highly doubt slightly higher interest expense on loans is going to stop the excess demand.</p> <p><strong>Inflation is being caused by factors the Fed cannot impact</strong></p> <p>Friday’s CPI report showed inflation at 3.4%, but the breakdown of categories reveals it is almost entirely energy prices (mostly oil).</p> <p><figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><picture> <span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"640\" data-og-image-facebook=\"true\" data-og-image-google_news=\"true\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"true\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"true\" data-og-image-msn=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"true\" data-width=\"727\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474208806_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"A graph of a bar chart AI-generated content may be incorrect.\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"563\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474208806_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474208806_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474208806_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474208806_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474208806_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"><span>BLS</span></p></figcaption></figure></p> <p>Excluding food and energy, CPI was only 2.4% and that 2.4% includes the embedded cost of energy. In other words, every product costs more because shipping costs more.</p> <p>Removing energy and the cost of energy embedded in other items, CPI is essentially at 2%.</p> <p>Inflation currently is almost entirely related to oil prices and secondarily to electricity prices from the surge in demand for AI. From the BLS release:</p> <blockquote><p>“The natural gas index increased 4.4 percent over the 12 months ending in August, and the electricity index rose 3.8 percent.”</p></blockquote> <p>Rate hikes will not stop the oil supply shock or the AI demand surge.</p> <p>Inflation will calm down when oil normalizes whether the Fed hikes or not.</p> <p><strong>How to play it</strong></p> <p>Interest rate sensitive stocks have generally traded down in recent weeks as the odds of a Fed hike rose from 50% to 92.7%. The September Fed meeting is seen as a harbinger of higher interest expense to come.</p> <p>I think the sell-off was overdone as a 25-basis-point hike to the Fed Funds rate is unlikely to be reflected in the longer end of the curve. Aside from companies that depend on floating rate debt, a Fed hike could have minimal to no impact on cost of borrowing.</p> <p>REITs that sold off in anticipation of a Fed hike may rally on the actual news. In particular, we like those with strong balance sheets that don’t rely on variable rate debt.</p> <p><figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><picture> <span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"438\" data-og-image-facebook=\"true\" data-og-image-google_news=\"true\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"true\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"true\" data-og-image-msn=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"true\" data-width=\"1207\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474914613_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"A graph of a graph AI-generated content may be incorrect.\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"232\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474914613_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474914613_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474914613_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474914613_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/9/14/1843741-17894173474914613_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"><span>SA</span></p></figcaption></figure></p> <p>Equinix (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/EQIX\" title=\"Equinix, Inc.\">EQIX</a>), W. P. Carey (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/WPC\" title=\"W. P. Carey Inc.\">WPC</a>) and East Group Properties (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/EGP\" title=\"EastGroup Properties, Inc.\">EGP</a>) each sold off in the past month, but would be largely unaffected by a Fed hike. We anticipate recovery of this recent drop and view these companies as generally strong investments.</p></body></html>",
